[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
**Short answer:**  
Use a **`color-relief`** layer. Its `color-relief-color` paint property takes an **`interpolate` expression on the special `["elevation"]` expression**, so you can build a hypsometric ramp (blue → green → brown → white) completely on the client.

Below is a minimal, copy-and-paste-ready example that shows the whole flow:

```js
// 1️⃣  Add the DEM source (raster-dem, not a raster image)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // any raster-dem format
  tileSize: 256,
  // optional: encoding: 'terrarium' | 'mapbox' (default is mapbox)
});

// 2️⃣  Add a color-relief layer that tints the DEM by elevation
map.addLayer({
  id: 'hypsometric-tint',
  type: 'color-relief',          // <-- the layer type you need
  source: 'terrain-dem',
  paint: {
    // The ramp – change the stops to suit your own colour scheme
    'color-relief-color': [
      'interpolate',
      ['linear'],
      ['elevation'],               // <-- elevation in metres (from the DEM)
      // ---- elevation (m) ----   ---- colour ----
      0,    '#2c7bb6',   // deep ocean blue
      50,   '#3b8cc4',   // shallow water
      200,  '#5fa5d0',   // lowland sea-level green-blue
      500,  '#7fc97f',   // low hills – light green
      1000, '#c2e699',   // mid-elevation – yellow-green
      1500, '#ffffbf',   // sub-alpine – pale yellow
      2000, '#fdae61',   // alpine – orange-brown
      2500, '#d73027',   // high rock – red-brown
      3000, '#ffffff'    // snow-capped peaks – white
    ],
    // Optional: make the tint semi-transparent so you can still see a basemap underneath
    'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
  }
});
```

---

## Why `color-relief` and not something else?

| Goal | Correct layer / API | What happens if you use the wrong one |
|------|---------------------|---------------------------------------|
| **Shade a DEM** | `hillshade` layer | Produces black-/white hillshade, not colour-by-elevation. |
| **Colour by elevation (hypsometric tint)** | **`color-relief`** layer | A `raster` layer will just show the raw DEM pixel values (packed RGB). `raster-color` / `raster-value` are *Mapbox*-only and are ignored by MapLibre. |
| **Draw contour lines** | `maplibre-contour` library (vector source) | Not needed for a colour ramp. |
| **3-D terrain** | `map.setTerrain()` + camera pitch | Independent of colour-relief; you can combine them, but the colour comes from `color-relief`. |

`color-relief` was added in **MapLibre GL JS 5.6** (and Mapbox GL JS 5.6). It reads the same `raster-dem` source that you would use for `setTerrain()` or `hillshade`, but it **does not require any pre-rendered coloured tiles** – the colourisation happens on the GPU at render time.

---

## How the expression works

* `["elevation"]` – a *special* expression that returns the elevation (in metres) of the current DEM pixel. It is **only valid inside** the `color-relief-color` property.
* `["interpolate", ["linear"], ["elevation"], …]` – linearly interpolates between the stops you provide.
* The stops are **pairs**: `<elevation-value>, <hex-or-rgba-colour>`. You can add as many as you like; the browser will smoothly blend between them.

> **Tip:** If you want a smoother transition at sea level you can add a few extra stops between 0 m and 200 m (e.g. 0 m → dark blue, 20 m → lighter blue, 100 m → turquoise, …). The more stops you add, the more control you have over the exact hue at each band.

---

## Optional extras

| Feature | How to enable |
|---------|---------------|
| **Combine with hillshade** (soft shading under the colour) | Add a `hillshade` layer *below* the `color-relief` layer (or above, depending on visual preference). Use `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` to avoid harsh black-white contrast. |
| **Add a basemap underneath** | Place a raster or vector basemap layer *below* the `color-relief` layer and set `color-relief-opacity` < 1. |
| **Adjust exaggeration** (makes the colour ramp appear “steeper” when you also have 3-D terrain) | `map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1.5 })`. The colour ramp itself is unaffected; only the geometry changes. |
| **Add a sky/fog** (so the horizon isn’t just background colour) | `map.setSky({ 'sky-color': '#199EF3', 'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5, 'fog-color': '#c0d8ff' })`. Works only when terrain is enabled (`setTerrain`). |
| **Query the elevation at a point** | `map.queryTerrainElevation([lng, lat])` – useful for tooltips or pop-ups. |

---

## Full minimal example (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Hypsometric colour-relief</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@latest/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [0, 0],
    zoom: 2,
    pitch: 45,          // tilt so you can see the terrain (optional)
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',
      tileSize: 256,
      // encoding: 'terrarium' // uncomment if your DEM uses Terrarium encoding
    });

    // Optional: a low-resolution basemap underneath
    map.addSource('basemap', {
      type: 'raster',
      url: 'https://tiles.example.com/satellite/{z}/{x}/{y}.jpg',
      tileSize: 256
    });
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'basemap',
      type: 'raster',
      source: 'basemap',
      paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.7 }
    });

    // Hypsometric colour-relief
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hypsometric',
      type: 'color-relief',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'color-relief-color': [
          'interpolate',
          ['linear'],
          ['elevation'],
          0,    '#2c7bb6',
          50,   '#3b8cc4',
          200,  '#5fa5d0',
          500,  '#7fc97f',
          1000, '#c2e699',
          1500, '#ffffbf',
          2000, '#fdae61',
          2500, '#d73027',
          3000, '#ffffff'
        ],
        'color-relief-opacity': 0.85
      }
    });

    // Optional: 3-D terrain (makes the surface extrude)
    map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1 });

    // Optional: sky/fog so the horizon isn’t just a blank colour
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#199EF3',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-color': '#c0d8ff',
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

---

### Recap

1. **Add a `raster-dem` source** (your existing DEM tiles).  
2. **Add a layer of type `color-relief`** that points at that source.  
3. In `paint → color-relief-color`, use an **`interpolate` expression on `["elevation"]`** to define the hypsometric colour ramp you want (blue → green → brown → white).  
4. Optionally tweak `color-relief-opacity`, combine with a `hillshade` layer, enable 3-D terrain (`setTerrain`) and a sky (`setSky`) for a full-featured relief map.

That’s all you need to get a client-side hypsometric tint from a raster-DEM source in MapLibre GL JS. Happy mapping!

**Short answer:** Yes.  
MapLibre GL JS can draw contour lines directly from a **`raster-dem`** source at run-time, but the core library does not do the contour-generation itself. You use the companion library **`maplibre-contour`** (by onthegomap) which runs a Web-Worker, extracts the elevation values from the DEM tiles, builds vector contour tiles on-the-fly, and serves them to the map through a custom protocol URL.

Below is a complete, step-by-step guide that shows how to:

1. **Add a raster-DEM source** (the same one you would use for hillshade/terrain).  
2. **Create a `DemSource`** from `maplibre-contour`.  
3. **Register the source with the map** (`setupMaplibre`).  
4. **Add a vector source that points to the generated contour protocol URL**.  
5. **Add a line (and optional label) layer** that draws the contours.  

---

## 1️⃣ Install the library

```bash
# using npm / yarn / pnpm
npm install maplibre-contour   # or yarn add maplibre-contour
```

> The library works with MapLibre GL JS ≥ 2.0 (the version that ships the `raster-dem` source).  
> It bundles a small Web-Worker; you can let it spawn a worker (`worker: true`) or run it on the main thread (`worker: false`) – the worker is recommended for smooth UI.

---

## 2️⃣ Basic map set-up (including the DEM source)

```js
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';
import mlcontour from 'maplibre-contour';

const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any base style
  center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
  zoom: 12,
  pitch: 45,               // optional – you can see terrain if you also enable it
  antialias: true
});

// Add the DEM source that will feed both terrain/hillshade *and* contours
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp', // <-- your DEM tiles
  tileSize: 256,
  // encoding must match the DEM you serve:
  //   "mapbox" (default) – 16-bit signed integer in RGB
  //   "terrarium" – 16-bit unsigned integer in RGB (offset 32768)
  //   "heightmap" – 8-bit grayscale (rare)
  //   "raw" – raw 16-bit little-endian (rare)
  //   "mapbox" is the most common for Mapbox-style DEMs.
  //   If you are not sure, check the source’s documentation.
});
```

> **Why add the DEM as a `raster-dem` source?**  
> The same source can be used for `map.setTerrain()`, a `hillshade` layer, a `color-relief` layer, **and** the contour generator. No extra tileset is required.

---

## 3️⃣ Create the `DemSource` (the contour engine)

```js
// 1️⃣ Create the contour “source” – this is NOT a MapLibre source yet.
const demSource = new mlcontour.DemSource({
  // URL pattern must match the DEM source you added above.
  url: 'https://tiles.mapterhorn.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.webp',

  // Must be the same encoding you used for the raster-dem source.
  // Options: "mapbox", "terrarium", "heightmap", "raw"
  encoding: 'mapbox',

  // The highest zoom level you expect to request contours for.
  // The library will request DEM tiles up to this zoom.
  maxzoom: 12,

  // Whether to run the heavy lifting in a Web Worker.
  // `true` (default) is recommended for any non-trivial map.
  worker: true
});

// 2️⃣ Register the contour engine with the map instance.
//    This adds a custom protocol handler (`mlcontour://`) that the
//    vector source will later use.
demSource.setupMaplibre(map);
```

> **What does `setupMaplibre` do?**  
> It tells MapLibre to treat URLs that start with `mlcontour://` as “virtual vector tiles” generated on-the-fly from the DEM. The library internally creates a `Worker` (if you asked for one) that reads the DEM tiles, runs a marching-squares algorithm, and returns GeoJSON-like vector tiles.

---

## 4️⃣ Add a **virtual vector source** that points to the contour protocol URL

```js
// The `contourProtocolUrl` method builds a URL that encodes the
// contour parameters you want (intervals, attribute names, etc.).
// The returned URL looks like:
//   mlcontour://contours?thresholds=...&elevationKey=ele&levelKey=level&contourLayer=contours
// MapLibre will request it like any other vector tile URL.
map.addSource('contours', {
  type: 'vector',
  // The library can generate different contour densities per zoom.
  // You give it a map of zoom → [minorInterval, majorInterval] (in metres).
  // The library will automatically pick the appropriate interval for the
  // current zoom level.
  tiles: [
    demSource.contourProtocolUrl({
      // Example: at zoom 11 use 200 m minor / 1000 m major,
      //          at zoom 12 use 100 m / 500 m, etc.
      thresholds: {
        11: [200, 1000],
        12: [100, 500],
        14: [50, 200]
      },

      // Name of the property that will hold the *elevation* of the line.
      // You can pick any string; the default is "ele".
      elevationKey: 'ele',

      // Name of the property that marks *major* contours (the ones
      // you probably want thicker or labelled). Default is "level".
      levelKey: 'level',

      // The name of the source-layer that the library will emit.
      // You will reference this in the layer definition below.
      contourLayer: 'contours'
    })
  ],
  // The vector source itself does not need a maxzoom – the contour
  // engine respects the `maxzoom` you gave it when constructing the
  // DemSource. Still, you can set a reasonable upper bound.
  maxzoom: 15
});
```

### How the `thresholds` work

| Zoom | Minor interval (m) | Major interval (m) |
|------|-------------------|--------------------|
| 11   | 200               | 1000               |
| 12   | 100               | 500                |
| 14   | 50                | 200                |

- **Minor contours** are drawn for every *minor* interval.  
- **Major contours** are drawn for every *major* interval and get a `level` property of `1` (or any truthy value you configure).  
- You can also supply a single array (e.g., `[100, 500]`) that will be used for *all* zoom levels.

---

## 5️⃣ Add a **line layer** (and optionally a **symbol layer** for labels)

```js
// 5️⃣ Contour line styling
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-lines',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',   // matches the `contourLayer` name above
  paint: {
    // Light gray for minor contours, darker for major.
    // `level` is 1 on major contours, 0 on minor.
    'line-color': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, '#222222',   // major
      '#888888'       // minor (default)
    ],
    // Thicker line for major contours.
    'line-width': [
      'match',
      ['get', 'level'],
      1, 1.5,          // major
      0.8             // minor
    ],
    'line-opacity': 0.8
  }
});

// Optional: label the major contours with their elevation.
map.addLayer({
  id: 'contour-labels',
  type: 'symbol',
  source: 'contours',
  'source-layer': 'contours',
  filter: ['==', ['get', 'level'], 1], // only major contours
  layout: {
    'symbol-placement': 'line',
    'text-field': ['to-string', ['get', 'ele']], // elevation in metres
    'text-font': ['Open Sans Regular', 'Arial Unicode MS Regular'],
    'text-size': 10,
    'text-allow-overlap': false,
    'text-ignore-placement': false,
    'text-rotate-alignment': 'map',
    'text-pitch-alignment': 'viewport'
  },
  paint: {
    'text-color': '#222222',
    'text-halo-color': '#ffffff',
    'text-halo-width': 1,
    'text-opacity': 0.9
  }
});
```

**Result:** As you pan/zoom the map, the worker reads the needed DEM tiles, computes the contour geometry, and the line/symbol layers render instantly. No pre-generated vector tileset is required.

---

## 6️⃣ (Optional) Combine with other elevation visualisations

Because the same `raster-dem` source is used, you can also:

| Goal | Layer / API | Example |
|------|-------------|---------|
| **Hillshade** (soft, multi-directional) | `type: "hillshade"` | see the skill doc for `hillshade-method: "multidirectional"` |
| **Hypsometric tint** (color-relief) | `type: "color-relief"` | use `["elevation"]` expression |
| **3-D terrain** (extruded mesh) | `map.setTerrain({source: "terrain-dem", exaggeration: 1})` | remember to set `pitch > 0` |
| **Sky / fog** (to hide the empty horizon) | `map.setSky({ ... })` | only works when terrain is on |

All of these can coexist; just be mindful of layer order (hillshade usually goes **below** any raster imagery, color-relief goes **above** hillshade, and contour lines typically go **above** both).

---

## 7️⃣ Performance & practical tips

| Issue | Why it happens | How to mitigate |
|-------|----------------|-----------------|
| **Stutter on low-end devices** | Contour generation is CPU-intensive, especially at high zoom where many DEM tiles are needed. | • Keep `maxzoom` modest (e.g., 12-13). <br>• Use the worker (`worker: true`). <br>• Limit the number of zoom levels that request dense contours (use larger intervals at high zoom). |
| **Missing contours** | The DEM tiles for the requested area/zoom are not available (404) or the `encoding` is wrong. | Verify the DEM URL works directly in the browser. <br>Check the `encoding` option matches the tile format (Mapbox, Terrarium, etc.). |
| **Contours look “jagged”** | The DEM resolution is coarse or the contour interval is too small. | Increase the interval (e.g., use 200 m instead of 50 m) or use a higher-resolution DEM source. |
| **Too many vector tiles** | Each contour request creates a new virtual tile URL; the worker caches results, but the browser may still request many tiles while panning fast. | Enable `worker: true` (caching happens inside the worker). <br>Set `maxzoom` on the vector source to a reasonable value (e.g., 15). |
| **Label overlap** | Many major contours close together cause label collisions. | Adjust `text-size`, `text-allow-overlap`, or use a `text-max-angle`/`text-keep-upright` layout property. |

---

